tel 1.0

base apoplexy
import soundness

info   typed HTTP clients derived from an OpenAPI specification as the code compiles
detail
    _Apoplexy_ turns an [OpenAPI](https://www.openapis.org/) document — the paths, parameters,
    request and response schemas and security schemes of a REST API — into a **typed client**
    without generating any code. `Api(cp"/spec.json")` reads the specification from the
    classpath while the code compiles, and the value it returns navigates by path: each
    segment, each path parameter, each query or header parameter, each request body and each
    response type is checked against the document, so a call the API does not offer, or a
    parameter of the wrong type, is a compile error where the call is written. The client *is*
    the specification, interpreted by the compiler, and drift between code and contract fails
    the build rather than a request.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import strategies.throwUnsafely
    import internetAccess.online
    import logging.silentLogging
    import classloaders.threadContextClassloader
    import construables.jsonConstruable
    import postables.jsonPostable
    import codepages.utf8Codepage
    import charsets.utf8Charset
    import formatting.compactJsonFormatting
    import httpBackends.javaNetHttp

    case class Pet(id: Int, name: Text, tag: Optional[Text] = Unset)
    case class NewPet(name: Text, tag: Optional[Text] = Unset)

    val api = Api(cp"/openapi/local/petstore.json")

    api.pets(42).get.call[Pet]()                      // GET /pets/42, decoded by the schema
    api.pets.get(limit = 10).call[List[Pet]]()        // a query parameter
    api.pets.post(NewPet(t"Milo", tag = t"cat")).call[Pet]()   // a JSON request body
    api.sessions(t"abc").delete.call()                // 204, no body: Unit
    api.pets(42).get.record().name                    // a record typed by the schema
    ```

    Three groups of types make up the library. `Api` is the navigable client: a value refined
    with its position in the API (`Api at "/pets/{petId}"`) and the wire format the
    specification declares (`Api over Json`), whose `Dynamic` members the compiler resolves
    against the document; invoking a verb on it gives an `Api.Response`, whose `call`,
    `attempt`, `record` and `tuple` perform the request. `Api.Error` is the family of errors a
    response outside the success range raises, one class per status, and `Api.Violation` is a
    status the specification never declared. `OpenApi` is the document itself as a value — info,
    servers, paths, operations, parameters, bodies, responses, components and security
    schemes — decoded from JSON or YAML with `read[OpenApi]`, for tooling that inspects an API
    rather than calling it; `OpenApi.Error` names what is wrong with a document that will not
    load.

    How a response body becomes a value is decided by its **media type**. The specification
    names the media type of each body, and a `gesticulate.Construable` given — `("application/json"
    is Construable to Json)`, `("image/png" is Construable to (Raster in Png))` — names the
    Soundness type which carries it. Every such mapping is a given in the `construables`
    family, declared by the format's own library (`jacinta.http` for JSON, `xylophone.http`
    for XML, `hallucination` for the raster formats, `gesticulate` for plain text and octet
    streams, …) and **imported by name**, since `import soundness.*` does not import givens:
    nothing is construed without a deliberate choice, and a response whose media type nothing
    in scope construes is read as the raw `Http.Response`, with a compile-time warning. A
    request body is written by the carrier's `telekinesis.Postable`, likewise imported by name
    from `postables`. The `Conformant` typeclass joins the two: `call[T]()` reads the carrier
    and decodes `T` from it, so any `T` with a `Decodable in Json` can be asked for from a JSON
    response.

    Credentials come from `orthodoxy`: a `Credential` given named for the security scheme
    (`("api_key" is Credential to Text)`, `("basicAuth" is Credential to Auth)`,
    `("petstore_auth" is Credential to Authorization)`) is summoned where the call is written
    and presented as the scheme dictates — a header, a query parameter, a cookie or a `Bearer`
    authorization — and an operation whose requirements no credentials in scope satisfy does
    not compile, the error naming the givens which would.

    The library has one source set, `apoplexy.core`, on the JVM only; its macros read the
    specification through the compiler's classloader, so the document must be a classpath
    resource named by the literal-only `cp"…"` interpolator. `Api`, `Conformant` and `OpenApi`
    are exported to the `soundness` umbrella; the top-level given `yamlEncodableInJson`
    is not, and is imported as `apoplexy.yamlEncodableInJson` where a YAML document is to be
    read as JSON by hand.

refer apoplexy.Api
refer apoplexy.OpenApi
refer apoplexy.Conformant
refer gesticulate.Construable
refer orthodoxy.Credential

entry .Api   a client navigated by path, each step checked against the specification
  detail
      An `Api` is a position in an API: the request so far (`#request`, an `Api.Request`
      holding the method, base URL, path template, substitutions, query, headers, body and
      `accept` media type) together with a refined type which records the position as the
      literal `Locus` (`Api at "/pets/{petId}"`), the specification resource as `Source`, and
      the wire format the document's responses share as `Transport` (`Api over Json`, or
      `over Xml`). Every member access is `Dynamic`: `#selectDynamic`, `#applyDynamic` and
      `#applyDynamicNamed` are macros which re-read the specification and halt the
      compilation when the path, parameter or verb is not there.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val api = Api(cp"/openapi/local/petstore.json")

      val pets: Api at "/pets" = api.pets
      val one: Api at "/pets/{petId}" = api.pets(42)
      api.pets(42).photos.request.path          // t"/pets/{petId}/photos"
      api.unicorns                              // does not compile
      api.pets(t"notAnInt")                     // does not compile
      ```

      Navigation follows four rules. A **segment** is a member access, `api.pets`; a segment
      the specification writes with characters Scala does not allow in an identifier goes in
      backticks, `` api.`museum-hours` `` or `` api.users.`@me` ``. A **path parameter** is a
      positional argument on the segment before it, `api.pets(42)`, typed by the parameter's
      schema: `type: integer` is an `Int`, `format: int64` a `Long` (an `Int` is accepted
      too), `number` a `Double`, `boolean` a `Boolean`, and anything else `Text`. A **verb** —
      `get`, `put`, `post`, `delete`, `patch`, `head`, `options`, `trace` — on a complete path
      invokes the operation, with query and header parameters as named arguments and the
      request body as the one positional argument, and yields an `Api.Response`. An endpoint
      with exactly one operation other than `DELETE` may be invoked without its verb, by
      applying arguments to the segment directly, `api.pets(42).photos(width = 10)` or
      `api.login(credentials)`; an endpoint with several operations needs the verb, and
      `DELETE` is always explicit. A required parameter left out, a parameter of the wrong
      type, a path item with no such verb and a verb-less application to a multi-operation
      endpoint are each compile errors.

      ```scala fuel
      frame
        label The root of the specification
        code
            val api = Api(cp"/openapi/local/petstore.json")   // Api at "/" over Json
      frame
        label A segment, checked to begin a path
        code
            val pets = api.pets                               // Api at "/pets"
      frame
        label A path parameter, typed by its schema
        code
            val pet = api.pets(42)                            // Api at "/pets/{petId}", 42 an Int
      frame
        label A verb with its parameters: an `Api.Response`
        code
            val response = api.pets(42).photos.get(width = 10)   // Api.Response over Json
      frame
        label The call, decoded as the type asked for
        code
            case class Photo(url: Text, width: Optional[Int] = Unset, height: Optional[Int] = Unset)
            val photos: List[Photo] = api.pets(42).photos.get(width = 10).call[List[Photo]]()
      ##
      ```

      Parameters declared on the path item apply to every operation on it, a parameter,
      response or request body written as a `$ref` into the document's components is
      followed, and a query or header parameter is rendered with its argument's `Showable`.
      Where an operation declares several success responses, `200` is preferred, then `201`,
      then the lowest.

      The base URL comes from the specification's first server, with its `{variables}` at
      their defaults, parsed as a URL as the code compiles; a relative server (`/api/v3`) or
      no server at all needs `Api(resource, base)`. The specification is read from the
      classpath through the compiler's context classloader, so the resource must be a
      `cp"…"` literal on the compiler's classpath, as JSON or YAML, OpenAPI 3.0 or 3.1.
      Cookie parameters, `callbacks`, `links`, `webhooks` and references into other
      documents are not acted on, and a `multipart/form-data` body is not construed.
  refer apoplexy.Api.Response
  refer apoplexy.Api.Request
  refer apoplexy.Api.Error
  refer apoplexy.OpenApi
  refer gesticulate.Construable
  entry .apply   `Api(cp"/spec.json")`, or `Api(resource, base)`: the root of a specification's client
      A macro which reads the `Resource`'s `Locus` — so the argument must be a `cp"…"` literal,
      whose existence the `cp` interpolator has already checked — loads and validates the
      document while the code compiles, and returns an `Api at "/"` carrying the resource's
      path in its `Source` type, for the navigation macros to re-read. Its `Transport` is the
      carrier type every operation's success response shares, through the `Construable`
      givens in scope at the call, or `Json` where the document mixes formats; each operation
      recomputes its own.

      The one-argument form takes the base URL from the specification's first server, with
      each `{variable}` at its default (`OpenApi.Server#resolved`), and halts the compilation
      if that is not an absolute `http` or `https` URL: _apoplexy: the server URL `/api/v3` is
      relative; supply a base URL_. The two-argument form takes the base from the caller; a
      relative server URL is appended to it with `Api.extend`, and an absolute one is replaced
      by it outright.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val local = Api(cp"/openapi/local/petstore.json")
      val remote = Api(cp"/openapi/local/refstore.json", base = url"https://ref.example.com")
      remote.request.base.show      // t"https://ref.example.com/v2": the base plus the server /v{version}
      ```

      A document that will not parse, declares a version other than 3.x or names a parameter
      location other than `path`, `query`, `header` or `cookie` is a compile error carrying the
      `OpenApi.Error` message.
  entry .extend   a relative server URL appended to a caller's base
      `Api.extend(base, server)` is the `HttpUrl` whose origin, query and fragment are
      `base`'s and whose location is `base`'s followed by `server`: what `Api(resource, base)`
      does with a specification whose server is `/api/v3`. The server text is appended as it
      is, so a base ending in `/` and a server beginning with one give a doubled slash.
  entry .make   an `Api` over a given `Api.Request`
      The constructor the navigation macros emit: a plain `Api` whose `#request` is the one
      given, cast afterwards to its refined type. It carries no refinement itself, so an `Api`
      made by hand cannot be navigated — the macros halt with _apoplexy: the receiver has no
      spec `Source`_ — but it can be sent with `Api.send`.
  entry .Records   the JSON reading primitives `record()` and `tuple()` build their values over
    hidden
    detail
        A `Json.Provider.Primitives` with no fields of its own: the `record()` and `tuple()`
        macros supply the fields of each response's schema, and read each member through these
        primitives from the response's JSON. Not for direct use.
    entry .fields   no fields of its own: the macros supply each response's from its schema
    entry .list   the records of an array response, one per element
        `Api.Records.list(json, transform)` splits a JSON array into one record per element,
        each built over the same primitives; how `record()` reads an array-of-objects
        response as a `List` of records. Raises `Json.Provider.Error` for a non-array.
  entry .apiKey   an API key as the header the scheme names
      `Api.apiKey(credential, name)` is `Http.Header(name, credential.value)`: how the
      invoking macro presents a `Credential to Text` for an `apiKey` scheme whose `in` is
      `header`. One for a scheme whose `in` is `query` becomes a query parameter instead, and
      `.cookieKey` handles `cookie`.
  entry .cookieKey   an API key as a `cookie` header, `name=value`
      `Api.cookieKey(credential, name)` is `Http.Header(t"cookie", t"$name=${credential.value}")`,
      the presentation of a `Credential to Text` for an `apiKey` scheme whose `in` is
      `cookie`. `Api.send` joins every `cookie` header into one with `; `, as RFC 6265 asks.
  entry .httpAuth   HTTP authentication as an `authorization` header
      `Api.httpAuth(credential)` renders a `Credential to Auth` — `Auth.Basic(user, password)`,
      `Auth.Bearer(token)` — as the `authorization` header, for an `http` scheme. A token
      obtained elsewhere may be presented this way for an `oauth2` or `openIdConnect` scheme
      too, when the credential is an `Auth` rather than an `Authorization`.
  entry .tokenAuth   an OAuth 2 `Authorization` as a `Bearer` header, checked against the required scopes
      `Api.tokenAuth(credential, scopes)` takes a `Credential to Authorization` and the scopes
      the security requirement names, and, before the request is sent, raises `OAuth.Error`
      with `Reason.InsufficientPrivileges(scope)` for the first scope the token does not
      grant; otherwise it is `Http.Header(t"authorization", …)` with the token's `bearer`
      rendering. It needs a `Tactic[OAuth.Error]` and `Diagnostics` in scope, which the
      invoking macro summons where the call is written.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val api = Api(cp"/openapi/swagger/petstore3.json", base = url"https://petstore3.swagger.io")

      given narrower: ("petstore_auth" is Credential to Authorization) =
        Credential(Authorization(t"tok-2", List(t"read:pets"), Unset, Unset))

      capture[OAuth.Error](api.pet.findByStatus.get(status = t"sold")).reason
      // OAuth.Error.Reason.InsufficientPrivileges(t"write:pets")
      ```
  entry .jsonOf   a response body read as `Json`
      `Api.jsonOf(response)` memoizes the body's stream and aggregates it as `Json`, raising
      `Parse.Error` for malformed JSON and `Truncation.Error` for a body cut short. `record()`
      and `tuple()` read their response through it, as do the error payloads which are records.
  entry .dataOf   a response body's bytes, memoized
      `Api.dataOf(response)` is the whole body as `Data`, needing a `Tactic[Truncation.Error]`.
      The invoking code uses it for the payload of an `Api.Violation`, whose body no schema
      describes.
  entry .send   performs an `Api.Request` through the `Http.Client` in scope, giving the raw response
      The runtime send which `Api.Response#call`, `#attempt`, `#record` and `#tuple` emit.
      The URL is the request's base with the path template's `{name}` placeholders replaced
      by their substitutions — each percent-encoded as a path segment, a space as `%20` — and
      the query attached, or the base's own query when the request has none. The headers are
      the `accept` media type the specification declares for the response, the `content-type`
      of the body, the request's own headers, and every `cookie` header joined into one with
      `; `. The request then goes as HTTP/1.1 through the `Http.Client onto Origin["http" |
      "https"]` in scope, which uses whichever `Http.Backend` is in scope, so a test can
      substitute a recording backend for the network.

      It needs `Online` (import `internetAccess.online`), an `Http.Event is Loggable` (such as
      `logging.silentLogging`), a `Tactic[Connect.Error]` for a failed connection, and the
      client. Nothing is checked against the specification here: a request built by hand is
      sent as it is.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import internetAccess.online
      import logging.silentLogging
      import httpBackends.javaNetHttp

      val request = Api.Request(Http.Get, url"https://api.example.com/v1", t"/pets/{petId}",
          substitutions = Map(t"petId" -> t"42"))

      Api.send(request).status
      ```
  entry .Body   a request body: nothing, or a media type with bytes sprung afresh for each send
    detail
        `Api.Body` is an enum of two cases. `Empty` is a request with no body, the default of
        `Api.Request`. `Content(mediaType, spring)` is a body of the `MediaType` the
        specification declares for it and a `Spring[Data]`, which yields the bytes anew each
        time the request is sent, so a retried request is not sent from a drained stream.

        The invoking macro builds a `Content` from the positional argument of a call: the
        argument is encoded to the media type's carrier through its `Encodable in Carrier`
        (`NewPet` to `Json` by `jsonEncodable`), or sent as it is when it already is the
        carrier — a `Query` for `application/x-www-form-urlencoded`, `Data` for
        `application/octet-stream` — and the carrier's `Postable`, imported by name from
        `postables`, writes the bytes. The `Postable`'s own media parameters are copied onto
        the specification's media type, so a JSON body sent with `jsonPostable` carries the
        charset the `Codepage` in scope gives.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*

        val api = Api(cp"/openapi/local/xmlstore.json")
        case class NewNote(text: Text)

        api.notes.post(NewNote(t"hi")).request.body match
          case Api.Body.Content(media, _) => media == media"application/xml"
          case Api.Body.Empty             => false
        ```
    entry .content   a body from a carrier value and its `Postable`
        `Api.Body.content[Carrier](mediaType, value)` makes a `Content` whose spring runs the
        `Carrier is Postable` in scope over `value` each time it is drawn. It is the helper
        the invoking macro calls, so that the stream is built outside the call site's
        capture checking.
    entry .data   a body of frozen `Data`, as `application/octet-stream` bodies are
        `Api.Body.data(mediaType, value)` is `content[Data](mediaType, value)` by a method whose
        signature names `Data` in ordinary code: the macro cannot write `content[Data]`
        itself, since a frozen type spliced through a quote is re-minted with a fresh read
        capability.
  entry .Request   the request a navigated `Api` or an `Api.Response` will send, as a value
    detail
        What `request` on any `Api` or `Api.Response` holds: the HTTP method, the base
        `HttpUrl` from the specification's server (or the caller's), the still-templated
        path, the substitutions which bind each `{name}` to its rendered argument, the
        `Query`, the body, the headers (parameters declared `in: header`, and credentials
        presented as headers or cookies) and the `accept` media type the specification
        declares for the response. Navigation copies and refines it one field at a time, so
        what is about to be sent can be inspected, or tested, before it is.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*

        val api = Api(cp"/openapi/local/petstore.json")
        val request = api.pets(42).photos(width = 10, height = 20).request

        request.method           // Http.Get
        request.path             // t"/pets/{petId}/photos"
        request.substitutions    // Map(t"petId" -> t"42")
        request.query.values     // List(t"width" -> t"10", t"height" -> t"20")
        request.body             // Api.Body.Empty
        ```
    entry #method   the HTTP method: `Http.Get` until a verb is invoked
    entry #base   the base URL: the specification's first server at its defaults, or the caller's
    entry #path   the path template as the specification writes it, `/pets/{petId}`
    entry #substitutions   the values bound to the path's `{name}` templates, each rendered as text
    entry #query   the query parameters, from named arguments and `apiKey` schemes sent in the query
    entry #body   the request body, `Api.Body.Empty` unless an operation took one
    entry #headers   header parameters and credentials presented as headers or cookies
    entry #accept   the media type the response is expected in, or `Unset` where none is declared
        The media type the specification declares for the success response, chosen as the
        first the `Construable` givens in scope construe (`application/json` first, then by
        name), and sent as the `accept` header.
  entry .Response   an operation ready to send: `call`, `attempt`, `record` or `tuple` perform it
    detail
        Invoking a verb on an `Api` gives an `Api.Response`, which holds the `#request` and a
        refined type recording the operation: `#Result`, a JSON pointer to the success
        response's schema within the document; `#Form`, the specification resource; `#Locus`
        and `#Verb`, the path and method; `Transport`, the carrier type the success response's
        media type construes; and `#Failure`, the union of the `Api.Error` types the
        operation's declared error responses raise. Nothing has been sent yet: `#call`,
        `#attempt`, `#record` and `#tuple` perform the request, and each needs `Online`
        (`internetAccess.online`), an `Http.Event is Loggable`, a `Tactic[Connect.Error]`, a
        `Tactic[Api.Violation]` and one `Tactic` for each member of `Failure`, and the
        `Http.Client` in scope.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely
        import internetAccess.online
        import logging.silentLogging
        import construables.jsonConstruable
        import httpBackends.javaNetHttp

        case class Pet(id: Int, name: Text, tag: Optional[Text] = Unset)
        val api = Api(cp"/openapi/local/petstore.json")
        val response: Api.Response over Json = api.pets(42).get

        response.call[Pet]()            // decoded as Pet, checked against the schema
        response.call()                 // the Json itself
        response.call[Http.Response]()  // the raw response, unchecked
        response.record().name          // a record typed by the schema
        ```
    refer apoplexy.Api.Error
    refer apoplexy.Conformant
    entry .make   an `Api.Response` over a given `Api.Request`
        The constructor the invoking macro emits, cast afterwards to its refined type. A
        response made by hand carries no refinement, so `call`, `record` and `tuple` on it halt
        the compilation; `Api.send(response.request)` sends it unchecked.
    entry #Result   a JSON pointer to the success response's schema within the specification
        `#/paths/~1pets~1{petId}/get/responses/200/content/application~1json/schema`, or a
        pointer into the components when the response is a `$ref`; `call[T]()` checks `T`
        against the schema it names, and `record()` builds its fields from it.
    entry #Form   the classpath path of the specification resource, as a literal type
    entry #Locus   the operation's path as the specification keys it, `/pets/{petId}`
    entry #Verb   the operation's method, lowercase: `get`, `post`, …
    entry #Failure   the union of the `Api.Error` types the operation's declared error responses raise
        A subtype of `Hazard`: `Api.NotFound[Unit] | Api.BadRequest[Problem]`, say, where each
        payload is the type the specification declares for that response. `#attempt` yields an
        `Attempt` over exactly this union, and `#call` demands a `Tactic` for each member.
    entry #request   the request this response will send, as an `Api.Request`
    entry #ensure   raises the declared error for a response outside the success range
        A macro which inspects the `Http.Response`'s status: a `2xx` status passes; a status
        the specification declares raises the `Api.Error` of that status (an exact status
        first, then a `4XX`-style range, then `default` as `Api.OtherError`), with the body
        construed as the payload type the specification gives it; and any other status raises
        `Api.Violation` with the body's bytes. The `Tactic` for each declared error, and for
        `Violation`, is summoned where the call is written, which is what makes handling
        exhaustive: a handler missing one is a compile error naming it. `#call`, `#record`
        and `#tuple` run this on every response before reading it.
    entry #call   performs the request and reads the response as the type asked for
        `call[T]()` first checks, as the code compiles, that `T` conforms to the response
        schema — a case class whose fields match the schema's properties, a `List[T]` for an
        array, `Json` or the raw `Http.Response` without any check — then sends the request,
        runs `#ensure` on the response and reads `T` through the `(T is Conformant) over
        Transport` in scope: the carrier's own `Aggregable` and `T`'s `Decodable in` the
        carrier, so `List[Pet]` resolves its decoder like any other `Decodable`. A `T` the
        schema does not support is a compile error.

        A bare `call()` leaves the type to `Defaulting to Transport`, which makes it the
        response's own carrier — the `Json`, the `Raster in Png`, the `Text` of a `text/plain`
        response — or `Unit` for a response which declares no body, as a `204` does, or the
        raw `Http.Response` when nothing in scope construes the media type.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely
        import internetAccess.online
        import logging.silentLogging
        import construables.{jsonConstruable, pngConstruable, plainTextConstruable}
        import httpBackends.javaNetHttp

        val refs = Api(cp"/openapi/local/refstore.json", base = url"https://ref.example.com")
        given queryKey: ("queryKey" is Credential to Text) = Credential(t"q-1")
        case class Item(id: Long, name: Text)

        refs.items(7).get.call[Item]()                  // decoded by the schema
        val icon: Raster in Png = refs.items(7).icon.get.call()
        val label: Text = refs.items(7).label.get.call()
        ```

        The empty parentheses mark the side effect. Besides the givens of `#request`'s send
        (`Online`, `Http.Event is Loggable`, `Tactic[Connect.Error]`, the `Http.Client`), a
        `Tactic` for `Api.Violation` and for each error in `#Failure` must be in scope, and
        the `Conformant` may need a `Charset` and a `Tactic[Truncation.Error]`.
    entry #attempt   the call as an `Attempt` over the declared errors, for an exhaustive `match`
        `attempt[T]()` performs `call[T]()` inside `contingency.attempt[Failure]`, so the
        result is an `Attempt[T, Failure]`: `Attempt.Success(value)` or
        `Attempt.Failure(error)` with `error` one of the operation's declared `Api.Error`s. A
        `match` over it is checked for exhaustiveness by the compiler, and a case for a status
        the specification does not declare does not compile; `recover` takes a partial
        function and is not. `Api.Violation` is not in the union: it is raised through its own
        `Tactic`, as the contract's breach rather than one of its cases. The type argument is
        needed explicitly when the transport is a record-bearing response.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely
        import internetAccess.online
        import logging.silentLogging
        import construables.jsonConstruable
        import httpBackends.javaNetHttp

        val refs = Api(cp"/openapi/local/refstore.json", base = url"https://ref.example.com")
        given queryKey: ("queryKey" is Credential to Text) = Credential(t"q-1")
        case class Item(id: Long, name: Text)

        refs.items(7).get.attempt[Item]() match
          case Attempt.Success(item)                        => item.name
          case Attempt.Failure(Api.NotFound(problem))       => problem.message
          case Attempt.Failure(Api.OtherError(_, problem))  => problem.message
        ```
    entry #record   performs the request and reads a JSON response as a record typed by its schema
        `record()` needs no mirror class: the result is a polyvinyl record with one member
        per property of the response schema, at the type the schema declares — `Text`, `Int`,
        `Long` for `int64`, `Double`, `Boolean`, nested objects and `$ref`s to component
        schemas as nested records, arrays as `List`s — and an array-of-objects response is a
        `List` of such records. Only for a JSON response whose schema describes an object or
        an array of objects: a response construed as anything else, or a schema of another
        shape, is a compile error suggesting `call[T]()`. It needs a `Tactic[Parse.Error]` and
        a `Tactic[Truncation.Error]` besides the send's givens, and raises
        `Json.Provider.Error` when a member read does not fit its schema.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely
        import internetAccess.online
        import logging.silentLogging
        import construables.jsonConstruable
        import httpBackends.javaNetHttp

        val api = Api(cp"/openapi/local/petstore.json")
        val pet = api.pets(42).get.record()
        (pet.id, pet.name, pet.tag)                    // (Int, Text, Optional[Text])
        api.pets.get(limit = 10).record().map(_.name)  // List[Text]
        ```
    entry #tuple   performs the request and reads a JSON response as a named tuple, eagerly
        As `#record`, but the schema's properties become the fields of a named tuple, in the
        schema's order, every one read when the response arrives rather than on access:
        `api.pets(42).get.tuple().name`. The same restriction applies: a JSON response whose
        schema is an object or an array of objects.
  entry .Error   a response outside the success range, raised as the error class of its status
    detail
        Error 914.1: _the server responded with an unsuccessful status, `status`_. The sealed
        parent of one case class per status telekinesis names — `Api.NotFound`,
        `Api.BadRequest`, `Api.TooManyRequests`, … — with `Api.Informational`,
        `Api.Redirection`, `Api.ClientError` and `Api.ServerError` for the `1XX`–`5XX` range
        keys (and for a numbered status telekinesis does not name) and `Api.OtherError` for a
        `default` response. Each carries `#status` and `#payload`: the error body construed as
        the type the specification declares for that response — a record typed by the error
        schema for a JSON object, else the media type's carrier, else `Text`, or `Unit` where
        no body is declared — so `Api.NotFound(())` matches a bodiless `404` and
        `Api.NotFound(problem)` one with a schema, whose `problem.message` is typed.

        An operation's declared error responses form a union of these classes, the `Failure`
        of its `Api.Response`; `attempt` yields an `Attempt` over exactly that union, and
        `call` demands one `Tactic` per member, so every declared error is handled or the
        code does not compile. Contingency tells the classes apart by class, not by payload
        type, which is why each status has a class of its own. The payload is covariant. A
        constrained member of an error payload's record — `string!`, `enum`, `pattern` — is
        relaxed to its plain type, since a raising member would make the error a capability.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely
        import internetAccess.online
        import logging.silentLogging
        import construables.jsonConstruable
        import httpBackends.javaNetHttp

        val refs = Api(cp"/openapi/local/refstore.json", base = url"https://ref.example.com")
        given queryKey: ("queryKey" is Credential to Text) = Credential(t"q-1")
        case class Item(id: Long, name: Text)

        refs.items(7).get.attempt[Item]().recover:
          case Api.NotFound(problem)      => Item(0L, problem.message)
          case Api.OtherError(_, problem) => Item(1L, problem.message)
        ```

        A status the specification does not declare is not an `Api.Error` at all but an
        `Api.Violation`.
    refer apoplexy.Api.Violation
    refer apoplexy.Api.Response
    entry #status   the HTTP status the server responded with
    entry #payload   the error body, construed as the type the specification declares for that response
  entry .Violation   a response with a status the specification does not declare
    detail
        Error 914.2: _the server responded with an undeclared status, `status`_. The server
        has broken its contract, which every call may meet and must handle apart from the
        declared errors: it is raised through its own `Tactic[Api.Violation]`, is never a
        member of a response's `Failure`, and carries the status and the body's raw bytes,
        since no schema describes them.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely
        import internetAccess.online
        import logging.silentLogging
        import construables.jsonConstruable
        import httpBackends.javaNetHttp

        val api = Api(cp"/openapi/local/petstore.json")
        case class Pet(id: Int, name: Text, tag: Optional[Text] = Unset)

        capture[Api.Violation](api.pets(42).get.call[Pet]()).status
        ```
    entry #status   the undeclared status
    entry #body   the response body's bytes
  entry .Continue   a declared `100 Continue` response, with its payload construed
  entry .SwitchingProtocols   a declared `101 Switching Protocols` response, with its payload construed
  entry .EarlyHints   a declared `103 Early Hints` response, with its payload construed
  entry .MultipleChoices   a declared `300 Multiple Choices` response, with its payload construed
  entry .MovedPermanently   a declared `301 Moved Permanently` response, with its payload construed
  entry .Found   a declared `302 Found` response, with its payload construed
  entry .SeeOther   a declared `303 See Other` response, with its payload construed
  entry .NotModified   a declared `304 Not Modified` response, with its payload construed
  entry .TemporaryRedirect   a declared `307 Temporary Redirect` response, with its payload construed
  entry .PermanentRedirect   a declared `308 Permanent Redirect` response, with its payload construed
  entry .BadRequest   a declared `400 Bad Request` response, with its payload construed
  entry .Unauthorized   a declared `401 Unauthorized` response, with its payload construed
  entry .PaymentRequired   a declared `402 Payment Required` response, with its payload construed
  entry .Forbidden   a declared `403 Forbidden` response, with its payload construed
  entry .NotFound   a declared `404 Not Found` response, with its payload construed
      The commonest member of an operation's `Failure`. `Api.NotFound(())` is a `404` the
      specification declares without a body; `Api.NotFound(problem)` one whose body the
      declared schema types.
  entry .MethodNotAllowed   a declared `405 Method Not Allowed` response, with its payload construed
  entry .NotAcceptable   a declared `406 Not Acceptable` response, with its payload construed
  entry .ProxyAuthenticationRequired   a declared `407 Proxy Authentication Required` response
  entry .RequestTimeout   a declared `408 Request Timeout` response, with its payload construed
  entry .Conflict   a declared `409 Conflict` response, with its payload construed
  entry .Gone   a declared `410 Gone` response, with its payload construed
  entry .LengthRequired   a declared `411 Length Required` response, with its payload construed
  entry .PreconditionFailed   a declared `412 Precondition Failed` response, with its payload construed
  entry .PayloadTooLarge   a declared `413 Payload Too Large` response, with its payload construed
  entry .UriTooLong   a declared `414 URI Too Long` response, with its payload construed
  entry .UnsupportedMediaType   a declared `415 Unsupported Media Type` response, with its payload construed
  entry .RangeNotSatisfiable   a declared `416 Range Not Satisfiable` response, with its payload construed
  entry .ExpectationFailed   a declared `417 Expectation Failed` response, with its payload construed
  entry .UnprocessableEntity   a declared `422 Unprocessable Entity` response, with its payload construed
  entry .TooEarly   a declared `425 Too Early` response, with its payload construed
  entry .UpgradeRequired   a declared `426 Upgrade Required` response, with its payload construed
  entry .PreconditionRequired   a declared `428 Precondition Required` response, with its payload construed
  entry .TooManyRequests   a declared `429 Too Many Requests` response, with its payload construed
  entry .RequestHeaderFieldsTooLarge   a declared `431 Request Header Fields Too Large` response
  entry .UnavailableForLegalReasons   a declared `451 Unavailable For Legal Reasons` response
  entry .InternalServerError   a declared `500 Internal Server Error` response, with its payload construed
  entry .NotImplemented   a declared `501 Not Implemented` response, with its payload construed
  entry .BadGateway   a declared `502 Bad Gateway` response, with its payload construed
  entry .ServiceUnavailable   a declared `503 Service Unavailable` response, with its payload construed
  entry .GatewayTimeout   a declared `504 Gateway Timeout` response, with its payload construed
  entry .HttpVersionNotSupported   a declared `505 HTTP Version Not Supported` response
  entry .VariantAlsoNegotiates   a declared `506 Variant Also Negotiates` response, with its payload construed
  entry .InsufficientStorage   a declared `507 Insufficient Storage` response, with its payload construed
  entry .LoopDetected   a declared `508 Loop Detected` response, with its payload construed
  entry .NotExtended   a declared `510 Not Extended` response, with its payload construed
  entry .NetworkAuthenticationRequired   a declared `511 Network Authentication Required` response
  entry .Informational   a declared `1XX` range response, or a `1xx` status telekinesis does not name
      Unlike the per-status classes, the range classes take the actual status as their first
      field: `Api.Informational(status, payload)`.
  entry .Redirection   a declared `3XX` range response, or a `3xx` status telekinesis does not name
      `Api.Redirection(status, payload)`; the actual status is its first field.
  entry .ClientError   a declared `4XX` range response, or a `4xx` status telekinesis does not name
      `Api.ClientError(status, payload)`; the actual status is its first field. A `404`
      declared exactly is `Api.NotFound`, not this, even when `4XX` is declared too: an exact
      status is matched before its range.
  entry .ServerError   a declared `5XX` range response, or a `5xx` status telekinesis does not name
      `Api.ServerError(status, payload)`; the actual status is its first field.
  entry .OtherError   a declared `default` response: any status no other declared response covers
      `Api.OtherError(status, payload)`, the actual status its first field. Stripe's
      specification, for one, declares every error as `default`, so every error from it is
      an `OtherError` whose payload is a record of the `error` schema.
  entry #request   the request so far, as an `Api.Request`
      The method, base URL, path template, substitutions, query, headers, body and `accept`
      media type the navigation has accumulated: inspectable before anything is sent.
  entry #selectDynamic   `api.segment`: a path segment, checked to begin some path of the specification
      A macro: `api.pets` joins `pets` to the current `Locus` and halts the compilation unless
      some path of the specification begins with the result (_apoplexy: no path begins with
      /unicorns_); the result is an `Api` refined to the new locus. A verb name (`get`, `put`,
      `post`, `delete`, `patch`, `head`, `options`, `trace`) which the current path item
      defines invokes that operation with no arguments instead, giving an `Api.Response`.
  entry #applyDynamic   `api.segment(x)`: a path parameter, or an invocation with positional arguments
      A macro. When `segment` is a verb the path item defines, the positional argument is the
      request body and the result an `Api.Response`. Otherwise `segment` is navigated as
      `#selectDynamic` would, and if the next segment of some deeper path is a `{template}`,
      the argument fills it — typed by the parameter's schema, rendered with its `Showable` —
      giving an `Api at "/pets/{petId}"`; failing that, the arguments invoke the endpoint's
      sole non-`DELETE` operation, as `api.login(credentials)` does.
  entry #applyDynamicNamed   `api.segment(name = x)`: an invocation with query and header parameters
      A macro. Each named argument fills the query or header parameter of that name declared
      on the operation or its path item, type-checked against the parameter's schema and
      rendered with its `Showable`; a positional argument among them is the request body. A
      verb invokes that operation; a segment with a sole non-`DELETE` operation invokes that
      one. An unknown name, a wrong type or a missing required parameter halts the
      compilation.

entry .OpenApi   an OpenAPI 3.x document as a value: info, servers, paths, components and security
  detail
      The specification itself, decoded from JSON or YAML with `read[OpenApi]` into typed
      parts for tooling that inspects an API rather than calling it: `#info`, `#servers`
      (with their variables), `#paths` of `OpenApi.PathItem`s holding `OpenApi.Operation`s,
      `#components` (schemas, parameters, responses, request bodies and security schemes) and
      the document-wide `#security` requirements. The `Api` macros read the same model to
      check each call.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import charsets.utf8Charset
      import classloaders.threadContextClassloader

      val document: OpenApi = cp"/openapi/local/refstore.json".read[Text].read[OpenApi]

      document.openapi                                          // t"3.0.3"
      document.paths(t"/items").let(_.get).let(_.operationId)
      document.servers.head.let(_.resolved)                     // t"/v2"
      ```

      A YAML document is translated to JSON (`yamlEncodableInJson`) and read by the one
      decoder, so the two forms cannot drift; which form the text is in is told by its first
      character, `{` for JSON. A parameter, response or request body written as a `$ref` is
      kept as an `OpenApi.Ref` in an `OpenApi.Referable` position and resolved on demand by
      `apply()`, given the document; a `JsonSchema.Ref` resolves the same way, one hop at a
      time, so cyclic schemas stay finite. The `responses` map of an operation is keyed by
      status text (`200`, `4XX`, `default`), which `response` interprets for a concrete
      status. A document that will not parse, declares a version other than 3.x or uses a
      parameter location other than `path`, `query`, `header` or `cookie` raises
      `OpenApi.Error`.

      The model is exercised against a corpus of published descriptions — the OpenAPI
      Initiative's examples, Swagger's Petstore, Redocly's Museum, Twilio, Kubernetes,
      Discord, Box and Stripe (1,538 schemas) — and loads GitHub's, OpenAI's and
      Cloudflare's. `callbacks`, `links`, `webhooks` and references into other documents are
      not modelled.
  refer apoplexy.Api
  refer apoplexy.OpenApi.Error
  refer jacinta.JsonSchema
  entry .decodable   reads an `OpenApi` from `Json`, with each nested part through its own anchored decoder
      The companion given `OpenApi is Json.Decodable`, derived once here so that `as[OpenApi]`
      does not inline the whole document graph at every call site. It needs
      `Tactic[Json.Error]`, `Tactic[JsonPointer.Error]` and `Tactic[OpenApi.Error]`.
  entry .aggregable   reads an `OpenApi` from text, JSON or YAML, checking the version is 3.x
      The `OpenApi is Aggregable by Text` given behind `text.read[OpenApi]` and
      `url.fetch().receive[Text].read[OpenApi]`: text beginning with `{` is parsed as JSON,
      anything else as YAML and translated; a parse, JSON, YAML or pointer failure becomes
      `OpenApi.Error(Reason.Malformed)`, and a document whose `openapi` does not begin with
      `3.` raises `OpenApi.Error(Reason.UnsupportedVersion(version))`. It needs a
      `Tactic[OpenApi.Error]`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val document = t"""{"openapi": "3.0.3", "info": {"title": "x", "version": "1"}}""".read[OpenApi]
      capture[OpenApi.Error](t"""{"openapi": "2.0", "info": {"title": "x", "version": "1"}}""".read[OpenApi]).reason
      ```
  entry .Info   the document's title, version and description
    entry #title   the API's title
    entry #version   the version of the API the document describes, not of OpenAPI
    entry #description   a description, or `Unset`
  entry .ServerVariable   a `{variable}` of a server URL: its default, its allowed values and a description
    entry .decodable   reads a server variable from JSON
    entry #default   the value `OpenApi.Server#resolved` substitutes
    entry #enum   the values the variable may take, or `Unset` when unconstrained
    entry #description   a description, or `Unset`
  entry .Server   a server of the API: a URL, possibly templated, with its variables
    detail
        A server URL may be absolute (`https://api.example.com/v1`), relative to where the
        document is served (`/api/v3`) or templated (`/v{version}`) with a
        `OpenApi.ServerVariable` per `{name}`. `Api` takes its base URL from the first
        server's `#resolved` form, and needs a caller's base when that is not absolute.
    entry .decodable   reads a server from JSON
    entry #url   the URL as written, templates and all
    entry #description   a description, or `Unset`
    entry #variables   the variables the URL's templates refer to, by name
    entry #resolved   the URL with every `{variable}` replaced by its default
        `/v{version}` with `version` defaulting to `2` is `/v2`. A template with no variable
        of that name is left as it is.
  entry .Ref   a `$ref` to a component, by JSON pointer, left unresolved in the model
    detail
        `OpenApi.Ref(pointer)` is what a `Referable[value]` position holds where the document
        wrote `{"$ref": "#/components/parameters/limit"}` rather than the component itself.
        `apply()` on the referable resolves it against the document in scope; only pointers
        into the document's own `components` resolve.
    entry #pointer   the JSON pointer the reference names
  entry .Referable   a component written in place, or referred to by a `$ref`: `value | OpenApi.Ref`
      The type of every position where OpenAPI allows a `$ref`: an operation's parameters,
      its request body, its responses, a path item's parameters and the components maps. A
      match must handle both: `case OpenApi.Ref(pointer) => …` and `case parameter:
      OpenApi.Parameter => …`; or `referable()` resolves either to the component, given an
      `OpenApi`.
  entry .Componental   which map of `OpenApi.Components` holds a kind of component, and under what name
    detail
        The typeclass behind `referable()`: for `Parameter`, `Response` and `RequestBody` it
        names the components map (`parameters`, `responses`, `requestBodies`) and looks a
        component up in it. The three givens are in the companion, so resolving a reference
        needs no import.
    entry .parameter   parameters live in `components.parameters`
    entry .response   responses live in `components.responses`
    entry .requestBody   request bodies live in `components.requestBodies`
    entry .apply   builds a `Componental` from a map's name and a selector over `OpenApi.Components`
    entry #kind   the components map's name, as it appears in a pointer: `parameters`, `responses`, …
    entry #lookup   the component of that name in the components, or `Unset` if absent or itself a `Ref`
  entry .apply   resolves a `Referable` component, or a `JsonSchema`, against the document in scope
      Two extensions, each needing a `given OpenApi` and raising `OpenApi.Error`.
      `referable()` on a `Referable[Parameter | Response | RequestBody]` is the component
      itself when written in place, else the one its pointer names under
      `#/components/parameters/`, `responses/` or `requestBodies/`. `schema()` on a
      `JsonSchema` resolves a `JsonSchema.Ref` one hop against `#/components/schemas/`,
      and is any other schema unchanged; resolving a hop at a time, never inlining, keeps a
      cyclic schema graph finite, with the consumer driving the walk. A pointer into
      anything else raises `Reason.UnsupportedRef(pointer)`, and one naming a component the
      document lacks `Reason.UnresolvableRef(pointer)`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import charsets.utf8Charset
      import classloaders.threadContextClassloader

      val document = cp"/openapi/local/refstore.json".read[Text].read[OpenApi]
      given OpenApi = document

      document.paths(t"/items").let(_.get).let(_.parameters.head).let(_()).let(_.name)   // t"limit"
      JsonSchema.Ref(t"#/components/schemas/Item".as[JsonPointer])()                     // a JsonSchema.Object
      ```
  entry .Parameter   a parameter of an operation or path item: its name, location, necessity and schema
    detail
        `in` is one of `OpenApi.Parameter.In`'s `Path`, `Query`, `Header` and `Cookie`,
        decoded from the document's text; a Swagger 2 `body` location raises
        `OpenApi.Error(Reason.BadParameterLocation(t"body"))`. A path parameter is always
        required; `required` is `Unset` where the document omits it. The `Api` macros type a
        path parameter's argument by `#schema` (`integer` to `Int`, `int64` to `Long`,
        `number` to `Double`, `boolean` to `Boolean`, else `Text`) and a query or header
        parameter's the same way, with `Text` where there is no schema.
    entry .In   where a parameter is sent: the path, the query, a header or a cookie
      detail
          An enum of `Path`, `Query`, `Header` and `Cookie`, decoded from the document's
          lowercase text by `.decodable` in its companion. The `Api` client sends path, query
          and header parameters; a cookie parameter is read into the model but not sent.
      entry .decodable   reads `path`, `query`, `header` or `cookie` from text, raising `OpenApi.Error` for anything else
    entry .decodableJson   reads a parameter from JSON, anchored so path items and operations share it
    entry .referable   reads a parameter-or-`$ref` position: a `$ref` key marks an `OpenApi.Ref`
    entry #name   the parameter's name, as the API expects it
    entry #in   where it is sent, an `OpenApi.Parameter.In`
    entry #required   whether it must be supplied, or `Unset` where the document is silent
    entry #description   a description, or `Unset`
    entry #schema   the `JsonSchema` of its value, or `Unset`
  entry .MediaTypeObject   the value of a `content` entry: the schema of a body in one media type
    detail
        A `content` map is keyed by media type text (`application/json`, `image/png`) and
        each value is a `MediaTypeObject` holding the body's schema, when one is declared.
        Its decoder is an anonymous given in the companion.
    entry #schema   the body's `JsonSchema`, or `Unset`
  entry .RequestBody   an operation's request body: its content by media type, and whether it is required
    entry .decodable   reads a request body from JSON
    entry .referable   reads a request-body-or-`$ref` position: a `$ref` key marks an `OpenApi.Ref`
    entry #description   a description, or `Unset`
    entry #required   whether the body must be sent, or `Unset` where the document is silent
    entry #content   the body's schema in each media type it may be sent as
  entry .Response   a declared response: a description and its content by media type
    entry .decodable   reads a response from JSON
    entry .referable   reads a response-or-`$ref` position: a `$ref` key marks an `OpenApi.Ref`
    entry #description   the response's description, or `Unset`
    entry #content   the body's schema in each media type it may come as; empty for a bodiless response
  entry .Operation   one HTTP method on a path: its parameters, request body, responses and security
    detail
        The `responses` map is keyed by status text as the document writes it — `200`,
        `201`, `4XX`, `default` — and `response(status)` on an operation interprets a
        concrete `Http.Status` against those keys. The `Api` client prefers `200`, then
        `201`, then the lowest `2xx` key as the success response, and raises a distinct
        `Api.Error` for each other key. `security`, when present, overrides the document's
        own requirements; an empty list means the operation needs no credentials.
    entry .decodableJson   reads an operation from JSON, anchored once so a path item's eight verbs share it
    entry #operationId   the operation's identifier, or `Unset`
    entry #summary   a short summary, or `Unset`
    entry #description   a description, or `Unset`
    entry #parameters   the operation's own parameters, each in place or as an `OpenApi.Ref`
    entry #requestBody   the request body, in place or as an `OpenApi.Ref`, or `Unset`
    entry #responses   the declared responses by status text, each in place or as an `OpenApi.Ref`
    entry #security   the operation's own security requirements, or `Unset` to inherit the document's
  entry .response   the declared response for a concrete status: exact, then range, then `default`
      An extension on `OpenApi.Operation`: `operation.response(Http.NotFound)` is the entry
      keyed `404`, else `4XX`, else `default`, else `Unset`, as a `Referable[Response]`.
  entry .PathItem   a path of the API: its operations by verb, and the parameters they share
    detail
        A path item mixes HTTP-method keys with `summary`, `description` and `parameters`, so
        each verb is its own `Optional[Operation]` field; `#operations` rebuilds the map by
        `Http.Method`. Parameters declared here apply to every operation on the path, an
        operation's own parameter of the same name and location overriding. Its decoder is
        an anonymous given in the companion.
    entry #summary   a short summary, or `Unset`
    entry #description   a description, or `Unset`
    entry #get   the `GET` operation, or `Unset`
    entry #put   the `PUT` operation, or `Unset`
    entry #post   the `POST` operation, or `Unset`
    entry #delete   the `DELETE` operation, or `Unset`
    entry #options   the `OPTIONS` operation, or `Unset`
    entry #head   the `HEAD` operation, or `Unset`
    entry #patch   the `PATCH` operation, or `Unset`
    entry #trace   the `TRACE` operation, or `Unset`
    entry #parameters   parameters shared by every operation on the path, each in place or as an `OpenApi.Ref`
    entry #operations   the operations present, as a `Map[Http.Method, OpenApi.Operation]`
  entry .Components   the document's reusable parts: schemas, parameters, responses, request bodies and security schemes
    detail
        What `#/components/…` pointers resolve into. `schemas` holds `JsonSchema`s, which a
        `JsonSchema.Ref` resolves against with `schema()`; `parameters`, `responses` and
        `requestBodies` hold components which may themselves be references, resolved with
        `referable()`; `securitySchemes` names the schemes an `OpenApi.Requirement` refers
        to. Its decoder is an anonymous given in the companion.
    entry #schemas   component schemas by name, the targets of `#/components/schemas/…`
    entry #parameters   component parameters by name, each in place or as an `OpenApi.Ref`
    entry #responses   component responses by name, each in place or as an `OpenApi.Ref`
    entry #requestBodies   component request bodies by name, each in place or as an `OpenApi.Ref`
    entry #securitySchemes   the security schemes by the names requirements use
  entry .SecurityScheme   how an API authenticates: an API key, HTTP auth, OAuth 2, OpenID Connect or mutual TLS
    detail
        The fields are those OpenAPI 3 §4.8.27 defines, and only the ones belonging to the
        scheme's `type` are present: `name` and `in` for an `apiKey`, `scheme` and
        `bearerFormat` for `http`, `flows` for `oauth2`, `openIdConnectUrl` for
        `openIdConnect`. `#kind` reads `type` as an `OpenApi.SecurityScheme.Kind`.

        The `Api` client satisfies a scheme with an `orthodoxy.Credential` given named for
        it: `("api_key" is Credential to Text)` for an API key, sent in the header, query
        parameter or cookie `in` names; `("basicAuth" is Credential to Auth)` for HTTP
        authentication, sent as the `authorization` header; `("petstore_auth" is Credential
        to Authorization)` for OAuth 2 or OpenID Connect, sent as a `Bearer` token and checked
        against the requirement's scopes (an `Auth` serves for a token obtained elsewhere). A
        credential of the wrong kind for the scheme is a compile error. Mutual TLS is not
        supported, and no OpenID Connect discovery is performed.
    entry .decodable   reads a security scheme from JSON
    entry .Kind   the scheme's type as an enum: `ApiKey`, `Http`, `OAuth2`, `OpenIdConnect`, `MutualTls` or `Unknown`
        `Unknown` is any `type` text the specification does not define; the `Api` client
        cannot satisfy such a scheme.
    entry .Flow   one OAuth 2 flow: its endpoints and the scopes it can grant
      entry .decodable   reads a flow from JSON
      entry #authorizationUrl   the authorization endpoint, for the implicit and authorization-code flows
      entry #tokenUrl   the token endpoint, for every flow but the implicit one
      entry #refreshUrl   the refresh endpoint, or `Unset`
      entry #scopes   the scopes the flow grants, each with its description
    entry .Flows   the OAuth 2 flows a scheme offers: implicit, password, client-credentials and authorization-code
      entry .decodable   reads the flows from JSON
      entry #implicit   the implicit flow, or `Unset`
      entry #password   the resource-owner password flow, or `Unset`
      entry #clientCredentials   the client-credentials flow, or `Unset`
      entry #authorizationCode   the authorization-code flow, or `Unset`
    entry #type   the scheme's type as written: `apiKey`, `http`, `oauth2`, `openIdConnect` or `mutualTLS`
    entry #description   a description, or `Unset`
    entry #name   for an `apiKey`, the name of the header, query parameter or cookie
    entry #in   for an `apiKey`, where it goes: `header`, `query` or `cookie`
    entry #scheme   for `http`, the authentication scheme: `basic`, `bearer`, …
    entry #bearerFormat   for an `http` bearer scheme, a hint at the token's format
    entry #flows   for `oauth2`, the flows the scheme offers
    entry #openIdConnectUrl   for `openIdConnect`, the discovery URL
    entry #kind   the `type` text as an `OpenApi.SecurityScheme.Kind`, `Unknown` for anything undefined
  entry .Requirement   a security requirement: the schemes which must all be satisfied, each with its scopes
      `Map[Text, List[Text]]`: scheme name to the scopes needed. An operation's (or the
      document's) `security` is a list of requirements, *alternatives* of which any one
      suffices, and an empty requirement means no credentials are needed. The `Api` client
      uses the first alternative every scheme of which has a `Credential` in scope.
  entry .Error   raised when a document is not valid OpenAPI 3, or a reference cannot be followed
    detail
        Error 846: _the OpenAPI document was not valid because `reason`_, with one of five
        reasons. `Malformed` (846.1), _the OpenAPI document could not be parsed_: the text was
        not JSON or YAML, or did not fit the model. `UnsupportedVersion(version)` (846.2),
        _the OpenAPI version `version` is not supported; only 3.x is supported_: a Swagger 2
        document, say. `UnresolvableRef(pointer)` (846.3), _the reference `pointer` could not
        be resolved_: a `$ref` to a component the document does not have. `UnsupportedRef(pointer)`
        (846.4), _the reference `pointer` is not supported; only #/components/schemas
        references work_: a `$ref` outside the document's components, such as into `#/paths`
        or another file. `BadParameterLocation(value)` (846.5), _`value` is not a valid
        parameter location; expected one of path, query, header or cookie_. `read[OpenApi]`
        raises the first two and the last; `referable()` and `schema()` raise the reference
        ones. At `Api(…)` any of them is a compile error.
    entry .Reason   why the document was rejected: `Malformed`, `UnsupportedVersion`, `UnresolvableRef`, `UnsupportedRef` or `BadParameterLocation`
        An enum, each case numbering its error and rendered by the anonymous `Communicable`
        given in its companion.
    entry #reason   the `OpenApi.Error.Reason`
  entry #openapi   the OpenAPI version the document declares, `3.0.3` or `3.1.0`
  entry #info   the document's `OpenApi.Info`: title, version and description
  entry #servers   the servers, in the document's order; `Api` uses the first
  entry #paths   the path items by path template, `/pets/{petId}`
  entry #components   the reusable components, or `Unset` when the document declares none
  entry #security   the document-wide security requirements, which an operation's own override

entry .Conformant   how an `Http.Response` is read as a value, through the carrier its media type construes
  detail
      `(T is Conformant) over Carrier` reads an `Http.Response` whose body is a `Carrier` —
      the type the response's media type construes, `Json` for `application/json`, `Xml`,
      `Text`, a `Raster in Png` — as a `T`. It reads only: `Api.Response#call` has checked the
      status, and `T` against the schema, before `#read` runs. The givens are layered by
      priority so that the result is never ambiguous: the raw `Http.Response` and `Unit` first
      (`.response`, `.unit`, for any carrier); then the carrier itself, read from bytes
      (`.carrier`); then the carrier read from text, for carriers such as `Xml` which are
      `Aggregable by Text` (`Conformant2#carrierText`); then any value `Decodable in` the
      carrier, by bytes (`Conformant2#decodable`) and by text (`Conformant3#decodableText`).
      The ordering keeps a carrier which is both `Aggregable by Data` and `by Text` (as `Text`
      is) from being ambiguous, and keeps a carrier from decoding through its own identity
      `Decodable`. The companion inherits those lower-priority givens from `Conformant2` and
      `Conformant3`, which it extends.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import charsets.utf8Charset

      case class Pet(id: Int, name: Text, tag: Optional[Text] = Unset)
      val response = Http.Response(Http.Ok, contentType = media"application/json")(t"""{"id": 42, "name": "Milo"}""")

      summon[(Pet is Conformant) over Json].read(response)    // Pet(42, t"Milo")
      summon[(Json is Conformant) over Json].read(response)
      summon[(Unit is Conformant) over Json].read(response)   // ()
      ```

      Because `call[T]()` summons the `Conformant` where the call is written, with `T`
      concrete, a `List[Pet]` resolves its `Decodable in Json` like any other decode, and the
      only givens demanded are the carrier's own `Aggregable` and `T`'s `Decodable`; the text
      paths also need a `Charset` for the bytes, and every path a `Tactic[Truncation.Error]`
      for a body cut short.
  refer apoplexy.Api.Response
  refer gesticulate.Construable
  refer distillate.Decodable
  entry .response   the raw `Http.Response`, for any carrier: the escape hatch which reads nothing
  entry .unit   `Unit`, for any carrier: the body is discarded, as for a `204` or a bare `call()` on a `DELETE`
  entry .carrier   the carrier itself, read from the body's bytes through its `Aggregable by Data`
      `(Json is Conformant) over Json`, `(Raster in Png is Conformant) over (Raster in Png)`:
      the body memoized and aggregated by the carrier's own reader. Needs a
      `Tactic[Truncation.Error]`.
  entry #read   reads the response as `Self`

entry .Conformant2   the middle priority of `Conformant` givens: a text-read carrier, and a value decoded from a byte-read one
  hidden
  detail
      A parent of the `Conformant` companion, which exists only to rank its givens below
      `Conformant.response`, `.unit` and `.carrier`; its members are found through the
      companion and need no import.
  entry #decodable   any `T` with a `Decodable in Carrier`, from a carrier `Aggregable by Data`
      How `call[Pet]()` reads a JSON response: the bytes aggregated as `Json`, then decoded as
      `Pet`. Needs a `Tactic[Truncation.Error]`.
  entry #carrierText   the carrier itself, read from the body as text through its `Aggregable by Text`
      For carriers such as `Xml` whose reader takes text: the bytes are decoded with the
      `Charset` in scope before the parser sees them. Needs a `Charset` and a
      `Tactic[Truncation.Error]`.

entry .Conformant3   the lowest priority of `Conformant` givens: a value decoded from a text-read carrier
  hidden
  detail
      The root of the `Conformant` companion's priority chain; its one given is found through
      the companion and needs no import.
  entry #decodableText   any `T` with a `Decodable in Carrier`, from a carrier `Aggregable by Text`
      How `call[Note]()` reads an XML response: the bytes decoded with the `Charset` in scope,
      aggregated as `Xml`, then decoded as `Note`. Needs a `Charset` and a
      `Tactic[Truncation.Error]`.

entry .Executor   the transport seam: turns an `Api.Request` into a raw `Http.Response`
  detail
      A trait with one method, `#execute`, for an implementation which answers an
      `Api.Request` without the network, such as a test's recording backend. It is not
      exported to the `soundness` umbrella, where its name would clash with superlunary's
      `Executor`, and is reached as `apoplexy.Executor`. The client itself sends through
      `Api.send` and the `Http.Client` in scope, so substituting an `Http.Backend` given is the
      usual way to intercept a request.
  entry #execute   the response to a request

entry .yamlEncodableInJson   a YAML document as JSON, as `yaml.in[Json]`
    The top-level given `Yaml is Encodable in Json`, the translation an OpenAPI document
    written as YAML passes through on its way to the model's one decoder. Both trees are
    walked node by node; a mapping key YAML wrote as a number or boolean (`200:`) becomes the
    string JSON requires. It lives at the package's top level with a library-qualified name
    because neither `jacinta` nor `ypsiloid` depends on the other, so neither `Yaml` nor
    `Json` can anchor it; it is not exported to the `soundness` umbrella, so it is imported as
    `import apoplexy.yamlEncodableInJson`.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import strategies.throwUnsafely
    import apoplexy.yamlEncodableInJson

    val yaml: Yaml = """openapi: "3.0.3"
                      |info:
                      |  title: Petstore
                      |  version: "1.0.0"
                      |""".stripMargin.tt.as[Yaml]
    val json: Json = yaml.in[Json]
    ```

entry .unsafeHttpUrl   parses text the `Api` macro has already validated as a URL, gated by `Unsafe`
    `unsafeHttpUrl(text)(using Unsafe)` is `text.as[HttpUrl]` with its `Url.Error` discharged:
    the code `Api(resource)` emits calls it on the server URL the macro has already parsed at
    expansion, gated by an `Unsafe` the macro supplies rather than a spliced `unsafely`, whose
    context function a quote cannot match under capture checking. Not for direct use: parse
    an unchecked URL with `text.as[HttpUrl]` and handle the error.

entry .Apoplexy   the macro implementations behind `Api`, its navigation and `Api.Response`'s calls
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use: the object exists so that the macros have a stable home. It caches
      each specification it loads, keyed by resource and content, for the length of the
      compilation.
  entry .root   the macro behind `Api(resource)`: loads the document and types the root
  entry .rootAt   the macro behind `Api(resource, base)`: as `.root`, with the caller's base URL
  entry .select   the macro behind `Api#selectDynamic`: a segment, or a verb with no arguments
  entry .applied   the macro behind `Api#applyDynamic`: a path parameter, a body, or a verb-less invocation
  entry .appliedNamed   the macro behind `Api#applyDynamicNamed`: query and header parameters, with a body
  entry .ensure   the inline entry point of `Api.Response#ensure`
  entry .ensureMacro   raises the declared error for a status outside the success range, or `Api.Violation`
      Summons a `Tactic` for each member of the response's `Failure`, and one for
      `Api.Violation`, where the call is written; dispatches on the exact status, then the
      declared ranges, then `default`; and construes each payload as the specification
      declares it.
  entry .record   the inline entry point of `Api.Response#record`, given the body already read as JSON
  entry .tuple   the inline entry point of `Api.Response#tuple`, given the body already read as JSON
  entry .recordMacro   builds the schema-typed record over `Api.Records` from the response's JSON
  entry .tupleMacro   builds the schema-typed named tuple over `Api.Records` from the response's JSON
  entry .check   the inline entry point which checks `call[T]()`'s `T` against the response schema
  entry .conform   halts the compilation unless `T` conforms to the response schema
      `Json` and `Http.Response` pass without a check; a case class is matched field by field
      against an object schema's properties, a `List[T]` against an array's items, and a
      primitive against its type, with `Int` accepted for `int64` and integral types for
      `number`.
